Package: com.andi.github-cli
Name: GitHub CLI (gh)
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: iphoneos-arm64
Maintainer: andi <tafilajandi@gmail.com>
Author: GitHub Inc. (upstream) / iOS port by andi
Section: Development
Priority: optional
Installed-Size: @ISIZE@
Depends: zsh, git, uikittools, firmware (>= 15.0)
Recommends: openssh
Icon: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/@REPO@/main/assets/icon.png
Depiction: https://@PAGES@/depiction.html
Tag: role::developer
Description: GitHub's official CLI, built natively for jailbroken iOS
 gh from the command line: pull requests, issues, releases, gists, Actions runs,
 repository management, and gh api for everything else.
 .
 This is a real ios/arm64 build of the upstream source, not a patched macOS
 binary. Go supports ios/arm64 as a first-class port, so the compiler emits an
 iOS Mach-O directly -- no Mach-O rewriting, no shim library, no JIT
 entitlements, and nothing downloaded at install time. gh is MIT licensed, so
 the binary ships in this package.
 .
 One difference from desktop gh: token storage. gh keeps credentials in the
 system keychain via /usr/bin/security, which iOS does not have, so it falls
 back to its config file at ~/.config/gh/hosts.yml -- the same path it uses on
 Linux without a secret service. Sign in as usual with `gh auth login`; the
 fallback is automatic and `--insecure-storage` makes it explicit.
 .
 gh extensions do not work, in either form. Script extensions fail at exec: a
 Go process on iOS cannot execve a file with a shebang. Binary extensions fail
 at install, because they publish darwin/linux/windows release assets and gh
 asks for ios-arm64. A hand-built, signed ios/arm64 binary extension does run,
 and gh's ! shell aliases work normally.
